A NOTE ON WHAT THIS ACTUALLY IS

Conditions 2 and 3 above are not part of any standard, court-tested
open-source license — most licenses either require share-alike (like the
GPL family) or don't touch commercial use at all (like MIT), but none of
the common ones bundle in an ongoing "share the proceeds" expectation,
because that's closer to a business arrangement than a licensing term.
What's written here is a clear, honest statement of what's expected, not
an automatically enforceable legal mechanism. For everyday use — other
researchers, students, hobbyists, people forking this for their own
similar project — stating it this plainly and publicly does the job. If
you're a company planning to build something commercial on top of this
and want the arrangement to be watertight, it's worth getting a lawyer to
turn condition 3 into an actual contract before you rely on it.

